rsetup-next and Cargo dependencies
==================================

The binary package is built with Debian 13 (Trixie) and Rust 1.85 or newer. CI
then installs and runs the resulting package in Debian 12 (Bookworm), which is
the runtime compatibility baseline.

The package build itself is network-isolated. For local and CI builds, run:

    git submodule update --init --recursive
    make deb-prepare
    dpkg-buildpackage --build=binary --no-sign

The first command checks out the pinned devicesinfo source in
externals/devicesinfo. Next links its crates/deviceinfo library as a Cargo path
dependency; it does not install or execute the upstream CLI. Automatic GitHub
source ZIP/tarballs omit submodule contents, so use a recursive Git clone.

The second command resolves Cargo.lock and fills debian/cargo-home. That ignored
cache is intentionally not part of the source package. Both preparation steps
must finish before the offline dpkg-buildpackage step.

The generated Debian packages are maintained and distributed only from the
rsetup-next repository. They are not intended for submission to the Debian
archive.

Debian 12's archive Rust 1.63 toolchain cannot compile this Rust 2024 workspace.
Bookworm is therefore a tested runtime target, not the source-build environment.
The final executable uses static C runtime linkage so it does not inherit
Trixie's newer glibc requirement.

Vendor marks
============

The Web interface embeds SoC vendor marks supplied from the Vamrs partner page
and a CIX reference image. Each mark remains the property of its respective
owner and is used only to identify the detected SoC vendor. The rsetup-next
project does not claim ownership of or relicense these marks.

Runtime dependency: xz-utils
============================

`rsetup-next system install` writes compressed `.img.xz` images by running

    xz -dc IMAGE

and streaming the decompressed bytes to the target block device. xz-utils is
declared in Depends, not Recommends, and the trade-off was decided explicitly:

- A compressed image is the format Radxa publishes, so this is the primary path
  of the system-install workflow rather than an optional extra.
- Recommends is skipped by `apt install --no-install-recommends`, by container
  and Dockerfile installs, and by minimal images. That would silently turn the
  main installation workflow into a runtime failure on exactly the headless
  systems this feature targets.
- The hard dependency is cheap: xz-utils is `Priority: standard` in Debian, is
  not a service, and is already present on an ordinary installation.

Absence is still handled without a crash and without a silent short write. The
preview path (`system plan`) parses the `.xz` stream header, index and footer in
Rust, so the consequence list, the uncompressed write size and the SHA-256
binding all work without xz-utils; only the write itself needs the tool, and the
preview already warns that it does. If `xz` is missing when the write starts,
the CLI prints the stable code `install_unsupported` with an actionable reason
instead of failing obscurely:

    env PATH=/usr/bin:/bin rsetup-next --demo system install \
      --target sda --image image.img.xz --acknowledge sda --json
    # {"error":{"code":"install_unsupported","message":"system image
    #  installation is unavailable: install xz-utils to write a compressed
    #  image"}}

That is the same explainable-reason style the action catalog uses for missing
commands (`Missing required command(s): ...`), expressed as a
`SystemInstallError::Unsupported` reason on the install path. The behavior is
covered by `debian/tests/smoke` and by the system-install checklist in
`docs/system-install.md`.

Unreleased changes and the changelog
====================================

The working tree carries the system-image installation feature set: core gates
and image validation, the CLI, the TUI panel, the HTTP routes, the Tauri
commands, the browser page, the new `xz-utils` dependency, and the install
guide. `debian/changelog` currently ends at the released `0.5.0-1` entry, and the
repository keeps no "unreleased" placeholder entry: every entry was written when
the maintainer chose the version (the `0.5.0-1` entry arrived in the same commit
whose subject names that version).

Choosing the next version number and its release date is a maintainer decision,
so no entry was invented here. Candidates to pick from:

- `0.5.1-1` - patch release: the same surfaces with a new runtime dependency and
  the packaged install guide.
- `0.6.0-1` - minor release: a large new user-visible capability (guided,
  irreversible system-image installation on four surfaces) plus that dependency.

The distribution (`bookworm`), urgency (`medium`), bullet style and trailer
format follow the existing entries. The trailer date must be the actual release
date, not the date this note was written.

Run-time directories under /run
===============================

`/run/rsetup-next` is a root-only parent for runtime work trees. The SPI boot
flash manager stages its private tree in `/run/rsetup-next/spi`
(`WORK_DIRECTORY` in `crates/rsetup-core/src/spi_flash.rs`) and creates that
directory on demand as root (`secure_directory` calls `create_dir_all` and then
sets mode 0700), so the tmpfiles.d rule is hardening rather than a hard
prerequisite: it keeps the mode at the declared 0700 instead of whatever the
caller's umask produces, and makes the tree exist before the first use instead
of appearing as a side effect. The rule and the postinst activation stay for
that reason.

Removed: the system-image install-token ledger
----------------------------------------------

An earlier revision of the system-image work claimed plan tokens in a ledger
directory (default `/run/rsetup-next/install-tokens`, overridable with
`RSETUP_INSTALL_TOKEN_DIR`) to make a token single use across processes. That
mechanism is gone: the package no longer ships a tmpfiles.d rule, an environment
variable, a postinst step or a smoke assertion for it. It was removed because the
two problems it had could not be fixed from inside it:

- the claim could not be authenticated. The ledger was shared state whose content
  any process able to write that directory could influence, and the token itself
  carried no integrity protection, so a rewritten nonce or issuance time could
  make a consumed token look new.
- it could not certify the property it existed for. "Single use across
  processes" is not provable from a directory whose writers are not
  authenticated, so the guarantee was weaker than the documentation claimed.

What the implementation does instead (`crates/rsetup-core/src/system_install.rs`):

- a token is signed with an HMAC-SHA256 key generated per process, so only the
  process that issued it can verify it, and editing any field fails;
- single use and the 600 second window are decided by the issuing process's own
  record of what it handed out, and consumption removes that entry in one step;
- a token is a **handle, not a credential**: the cross-process preview check
  (`verify_preview_token`) only notices state drift and age, and is explicitly
  advisory rather than an adversarial boundary;
- what protects the device is the rebuilt plan and its binding check, the hard
  gates, the typed device name, and the administrator authorization a real write
  requires.

A consequence for packaging: this feature needs no run-time directory, no extra
dependency and no tmpfiles.d rule, which is why this section replaces the earlier
"install-token ledger" one.

Not changed here
----------------

`/var/lib/rsetup-next/images` is the persistent, operator-populated image
catalogue the HTTP and desktop surfaces read. It is not ephemeral `/run` state,
so the package does not create or manage it.
